          ],
          "content": "For a hint, go to the end of the chapter."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.274,
            0.873,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "Make other artwork of your choice with a ruler and a compass."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.325,
            0.568,
            0.351
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.2 Squares and Rectangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.365,
            0.884,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "Now, let us look at some basic figures having straight lines in their boundary."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.41,
            0.747,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.494,
                0.619,
                0.557,
                0.637
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.3"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.655,
            0.884,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "What shapes are these? Yes, these are our familiar squares and rectangles. But what makes them squares and rectangles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.701,
            0.516,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "Consider this rectangle ABCD."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.723,
            0.603,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "The points A, B, C and D are the corners of the rectangle. Lines AB, BC, CD and DA are its sides. Its angles are $\\angle \\mathrm{A}, \\angle \\mathrm{B}, \\angle \\mathrm{C}$ and ∠D."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.814,
            0.603,
            0.905
          ],
          "content": "The blue sides AB and CD are called **opposite sides**, as they lie opposite to each other. Likewise, AD and BC is the other pair of opposite sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.626,
            0.718,
            0.888,
            0.857
          ],
